menu "Saans node"

choice SAANS_ROLE
    prompt "Node role"
    default SAANS_ROLE_RX
    help
        A Saans deployment needs at least one TX and one RX. The TX exists only
        to put packets in the air at a steady rate so the RX has something to
        measure the channel with; it does no sensing. The RX captures CSI, runs
        the DSP and emits FeatureFrames.

config SAANS_ROLE_TX
    bool "TX — ESP-NOW beacon source"
config SAANS_ROLE_RX
    bool "RX — CSI capture, DSP, FeatureFrame sender"
endchoice

config SAANS_NODE_ID
    int "Node id (0-254)"
    range 0 254
    default 0
    help
        Identifies this node on the wire. 255 is reserved for "unknown / an
        access point" in the tx_id field, so it is not a valid node id.

menu "Radio"

config SAANS_WIFI_CHANNEL
    int "WiFi channel"
    range 1 13
    default 6
    help
        TX and RX must agree. ESP-NOW is connectionless, so this is the only
        thing tying them together.

config SAANS_TX_RATE_HZ
    int "TX beacon rate (Hz)"
    depends on SAANS_ROLE_TX
    range 1 200
    default SAANS_CSI_RATE_HZ
    help
        One beacon out of here is one CSI frame at every RX on the channel, so
        this IS the rate the RX's DSP sees.

        SET IT TO THE SAME NUMBER AS SAANS_CSI_RATE_HZ ON THE RX, which is why
        it defaults to it. Nothing between the radio and the DSP decimates:
        csi_capture queues every callback and rx_role pushes every queued
        frame. Beaconing faster than the RX's configured rate does not buy
        headroom against loss — it makes the RX process more frames than it
        believes it is processing, so its sample clock, its vitals bands and
        its gap filler are all scaled wrong, and the link-statistics window
        overflows on top (the RX logs both). Beaconing slower is the case the
        gap filler exists for and is handled.

        The DSP needs >= 10 Hz: the gross-motion band reaches 4 Hz.

endmenu

menu "Sensing"

config SAANS_N_SUBCARRIERS
    int "Subcarriers to process"
    range 8 64
    default 56
    help
        HT20 reports 56 usable subcarriers (LLTF+HT-LTF). The DSP is sized at
        compile time from this, so raising it costs RAM whether or not the
        radio delivers them.

config SAANS_CSI_RATE_HZ
    int "Expected CSI rate (Hz)"
    range 10 200
    default 20
    help
        Nominal rate the DSP is configured for. The gap filler keeps the time
        axis uniform when packets are LOST, so this is the rate the pipeline
        believes in rather than the rate the radio happens to deliver — but
        that licence only runs one way.

        DELIVERING BELOW IT IS HANDLED. Delivering ABOVE it is not: nothing
        decimates, so every extra frame is processed as though it arrived at
        this rate, and the pipeline's clock, bands and windows are all scaled
        by the ratio. Set the TX beacon rate to this same number.

        It also sizes the 10 s link-statistics window (dsp/node_config.hpp
        derives the ring from it, with 25% headroom for arrival jitter). A
        radio delivering more than 25% above this rate overflows that window:
        csi_rate_chz and loss_permille are then computed over a shorter span
        than the spec's 10 s, and the extractor's arrival_overflows counter
        says so.

config SAANS_FRAME_RATE_HZ
    int "FeatureFrame rate (Hz)"
    range 1 10
    default 2
    help
        How often a 68-byte FeatureFrame goes to the gateway.

config SAANS_CALIBRATION_S
    int "Presence calibration (s)"
    range 10 300
    default 30
    help
        The space must be EMPTY for this long, after the filter warmup.
        Calibrating with someone present learns them as the baseline and the
        room then reads as permanently empty.

config SAANS_COMPUTE_BNR
    bool "Compute best_bnr (per-link quality)"
    default y
    help
        best_bnr is the highest per-subcarrier breathing-to-noise ratio over
        the last 30 s: a link-quality figure for system health, read by
        nothing else. No detector uses it.

        It is also the single most expensive field on the node. Its 30 s row
        store is 120 x 56 floats, 26.9 KB, more than half of what one link
        costs; turning it off takes a link from 45,408 bytes to 16,584 on the
        S3 (measured: edge/dsp/bench/run_size_report.sh --target).

        TURN IT OFF ON A NODE CARRYING MORE THAN 3 LINKS. With it on, 171 KB
        of free DIRAM holds three links and no fourth. With it off the same
        RAM holds ten.

        best_bnr then goes out as 0.0, which spec/wire.md defines as UNKNOWN —
        "before the first window, or on a memory-constrained node that does
        not compute it". Every other field in the frame is bit-identical with
        this on or off.

        0.0 IS AMBIGUOUS BY DESIGN AND THE GATEWAY MUST NOT READ LINK HEALTH
        INTO IT, in either direction. A node with this off emits 0.0; so does
        a node with it on whose window has not filled yet; and so does a node
        with it on watching a channel that does not vary at all, because a
        subcarrier with zero standard deviation is scored 0.0 and the field is
        the maximum over the subcarriers. spec/wire.md folds all of them into
        "0 = unknown" and there is no flag bit that separates them.

        This is a COMPILE-TIME choice: with it off, the row store is not in
        the binary at all, and the extractor refuses a configuration that asks
        for link quality rather than quietly reporting nothing.

endmenu

menu "Privacy (ADR-001, ADR-005)"

config SAANS_VITALS_ENABLED
    bool "Vitals enabled for this space"
    default n
    help
        OFF by default, and that default is a policy decision, not a
        convenience. Breathing and heart data are health data under India's
        DPDP Act 2023 and require opt-in consent per space. With this off the
        node writes 0.0 into every breathing and heart field, so the gateway
        never receives what it is not allowed to hold.

config SAANS_HEART_ENABLED
    bool "Heart rate enabled for this space"
    depends on SAANS_VITALS_ENABLED
    default n
    help
        Additionally gated. Heart rate from WiFi CSI is unreliable in a way
        breathing is not; see NOTES.md. Enabling it is a separate decision.

config SAANS_RAW_MODE
    bool "Also stream raw ADR-018 CSI (debug)"
    default n
    help
        Debug and data collection only. The raw_mode flag is set in every
        FeatureFrame while this is on, so a dashboard can show that the node
        is exporting raw channel data. That visibility is the point: raw mode
        must never be silent.

endmenu

menu "Gateway"

config SAANS_GATEWAY_IP
    string "Gateway IPv4"
    default "192.168.1.10"

config SAANS_GATEWAY_PORT
    int "Gateway UDP port"
    range 1 65535
    default 5566

config SAANS_WIFI_SSID
    string "WiFi SSID (RX uplink)"
    depends on SAANS_ROLE_RX
    default "saans"

config SAANS_WIFI_PASSWORD
    string "WiFi password (RX uplink)"
    depends on SAANS_ROLE_RX
    default ""

endmenu

menu "Reliability"

config SAANS_WATCHDOG_S
    int "Task watchdog timeout (s)"
    range 5 60
    default 15

config SAANS_TELEMETRY_S
    int "Telemetry interval (s)"
    range 10 3600
    default 60
    help
        Free heap, minimum-ever heap, uptime and reboot count. System health
        is a first-class output: a node that silently degrades is worse than
        one that reports it.

endmenu

endmenu
